## 2. Creating your first class

1. Click **"+ Create first class"** (afterwards, use **"+ New class"**, at the top of the class list, on the right side of the screen).
2. Give the class a name in the top field (e.g., *7A*, *Math — Grade 6B*).
3. Fill in the **School** field, if you'd like.
4. If this class takes part in a **Mapping Program**, check the "Class in Mapping Program" box — this unlocks per-student tracking described in section 6.
5. If you only started using the app from a term partway through the year, see **"Unrecorded previous terms"** — choose how many previous terms already happened, and a field to type in their grade manually appears in the Year Summary.

Each class automatically gets a different color. The class list stays on the **right-hand** panel of the screen, sorted by default by class number (7A, 7B, 8A...) — you can switch to alphabetical order in Settings. Each item has its own 🗑 button to delete that specific class.

---

## 3. Adding students

- **One at a time**: click **"+ Add student"** — their card opens ready to fill in right away.
- **Several at once**: click **"📋 Paste student list"**, paste the names (one per line — numbering/bullets are cleaned up automatically) and confirm.

Students always stay in alphabetical order. A search field above the list helps you find a name quickly in large classes — if the search finds only 1 student, their card opens on its own.

### Collapsible cards
Each student is a card that starts **closed**, showing just the name and the status light. Click anywhere on the header (outside the name and delete button) to open it and see everything: attendance, assignment, incidents, behavior, notes. **Attendance stays always visible**, even with the card closed — you can take the whole roll call without opening a single card. An **"Expand all / Collapse all"** button opens or closes them all at once.

### The status light next to the name
A colored dot appears next to each student: 🟢 doing well, 🟡 needs attention, 🔴 alert, ⚪ not enough data yet. It's calculated automatically, combining assignments turned in, behavior, and notebook/workbook/homework checks for the current term (checks still at zero don't count against the student — they only kick in once at least one has been logged).

---

## 4. Day to day: recording attendance, behavior and incidents

Right at the top of each class day, a green highlight: **"📌 I've already logged today's attendance in Sala do Futuro"** — a quick reminder to check off once you've entered attendance in the government system, so you don't forget. It's independent per day (checking it today doesn't affect other days).

Also at the top: the **class number** is now a counter with ▲▼ arrows (no more typing), and the **number of classes taught that day** is chosen with a button (1, 2, 3, 4) — handy for double periods. A counter shows how many classes have been taught this term, counting only days where attendance was actually filled in.

The **"Content covered in this class"** field got preset-phrase chips (Review, New content, Exercises, Assessment...) — one click fills it in, another adds to it, without erasing what was already there.

For each class day, mark for each student:

- **Attendance**: Present / Absent / Late / **Medical leave** — or use **"✓ Mark all as present"**. By default, a day with nothing marked already counts as Present (you can turn this off in Settings if you'd rather it stayed "unmarked" until you check something).
- **Term assignment** (Turned in / Pending).
- **Mapping Program today** (when applicable) — Yes/No, highlighted in yellow.
- **🚨 Incidents today**: Disrupting the class, Unauthorized absence, Side conversations, Sleeping in class, Using inappropriate language, Not in the Mapping Program (when applicable), Not doing the assigned activity, Did not bring materials, Inappropriate clothing, Left the room without permission, Refuses to do the activity, Brought a non-educational object, Inappropriate phone use.
  > ⚠️ By default, checking any incident (except "Not in the Mapping Program") **automatically lowers Behavior by one level** for that student that day. A day with no incidents counts as the maximum grade. You can turn this automation off in Settings if you'd rather always set Behavior manually.
- **Behavior** — one of 4 emoji (adjustable any time, even after an incident).
- **Notes** — free text, also with preset-phrase chips (Great participation, Effort in class, Trouble concentrating, Needs extra support, among others).

### Term records (don't change day to day)
Inside each student's card, a separate section — **"📓 Term records"** — holds three counters that apply to the whole term, not to a specific day:

- **No. of notebook checks**
- **No. of workbook activities done**
- **No. of homework done**

Each shows a live-calculated grade (proportional to the count, up to a maximum number that already earns grade 10 — adjustable in Settings). You can also check **"Student on home instruction"** right there: the regular Attendance/Absent/Medical leave/Late columns get nulled out for them (since those don't apply to someone on home instruction), but the classes-taught count keeps working.

---

## 5. Assignments, Assessments and External Exams

Three collapsible sections (click the title to open/close, each shows how many items are already recorded):

- **📌 Assignments to turn in** — check off who turned it in, student by student.
- **📝 Assessments** — grade from 0 to 10 per student, as many as you'd like per term.
- **🎯 External Exams** — same logic as assessments.

---

## 6. Class weekdays (makes attendance easier)

In the class header, check the weekdays you teach this class (e.g., Monday, Wednesday, Friday). From then on, every time you click **"+ New class day"**, the date is already suggested correctly — each new day advances to the next checked weekday, even if you create several at once, planning ahead.

## 7. Mapping Program

- Turn it on per class ("Class in Mapping Program", in the header).
- Within the class, mark each student individually as participating or not.
- The daily "Did they complete the Mapping Program today?" field and the "Not in the Mapping Program" incident use the same data — checking one reflects in the other.

---

## 8. Viewing summaries

- **Term summary** (inside "Term record"): **Behavior** shows as a grade + emoji (e.g., 🙂 8.0), and the **Notebook, Workbook and Assignment** grades also show as an average (0 to 10), alongside the usual counts. Each student shows their own **classes taught** count (adds up the number of classes on days they had attendance marked — stays blank if none were logged for them). Ends with the Term Grade and a **chart comparing each student's grade side by side**.
- **📅 Year Summary** (its own tab): one column per term (plus, if configured, extra columns for previous terms not recorded in the app, with a manually typed grade), total attendance, **classes taught**, and incidents for the year, the **"Sala do Futuro"** checklist, the **class progress chart** and the per-student performance chart. Terms with no assessment entered show a field to type in the average manually.

Both summaries have a **"📄 Export (Word / Google Docs)"** button.

